cat > "$TMPDIR/r820.json" <<'EOF'
{
 "commit_id": "10ea1f80a187e590e0e29e8fd16dd5099ea54acc",
 "event": "COMMENT",
 "body": "Reviewed on the PR head with the DeepWork review rules (code, docstring accuracy, doc sync, archy structural); each finding below was checked against the code. Structural checks are clean: no cycles, no layer violations, derived headers match, and every consumer of `parse_errors` (`cli.py`, `mcp_runs.py`, `mcp_payloads.py`) only counts or passes the strings through, so the mixed entry format breaks nothing in `src/`. The sentinel is never cached, as intended.\n\n**1. Doc drift: `docs/SPEC_GRAPH_MCP.md` line 60** still says `\"parse_errors\": [qualname, ...],`. Entries for unreadable files are now `\"qualname: OSError reason\"`, while tree-sitter syntax-error modules stay bare qualnames. Suggest: `\"parse_errors\": [str, ...],  # \"qualname\" for syntax recovery; \"qualname: reason\" for files that could not be read (#800)`. The type sketches at lines 97 and 124 (`tuple[str, ...]`) are still accurate.\n\n**2. Format change for consumers.** `parse_errors` was a list of qualnames and is now a list of two shapes. Nothing in this repo breaks, but an external MCP client that matches an entry against a module qualname will miss the unreadable ones. Either say so in the spec (item 1) or keep the list pure qualnames and carry the reason in a separate field. Your call; a note in the changelog is the minimum.\n\n**3. The reason string embeds the absolute path.** `f\"{type(exc).__name__}: {exc}\"` is `PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/abs/path/x.py'` in the common case, so absolute paths now reach `graph`, `score` and MCP payloads where entries were project-relative qualnames. I have not run it against a real mode-000 file to confirm the exact text. If the path is unwanted there, use `exc.strerror` (or the errno name) instead of `str(exc)`.\n\nOne inline note follows. Comment-only findings from the style reviewers were on lines this PR does not touch, so I left them out."
 ,
 "comments": [
  {"path": "src/archy/parser.py", "line": 212, "side": "RIGHT",
   "body": "Residual gap in the #800 fix: `Path.exists()` re-raises `OSError` for anything other than not-found-style errors (e.g. `EACCES` when a parent directory is not searchable). That lands here as `still_there = False`, so such a file is returned as `None` and dropped silently, the exact symptom #800 describes, just one directory up. Consider treating an `OSError` from `exists()` as \"present\" (the file did not demonstrably vanish) and returning the sentinel, and add a test with a mode-000 parent directory. Low severity: discovery may already fail to list that file."}
 ]
}
EOF
gh api repos/hslee16/Archy/pulls/820/reviews --input "$TMPDIR/r820.json" --jq '.html_url' 2>&1 | tail -3; git checkout main 2>&1 | tail -1

tools.description:
Run code in a sandboxed subprocess. (Bun detected — JS/TS runs 3-5x faster) Languages: javascript, shell, typescript, python, ruby, go, perl.

Think-in-Code — the core philosophy: the bytes your code processes never enter your conversation memory; only what you console.log() does. Reading a 700 KB log directly means 700 KB of your remaining reasoning capacity gets spent on raw bytes. Running code over that same log in this sandbox and printing a 3 KB summary leaves you with 697 KB of capacity for the actual work.

Concrete shape — analyze 47 source files without reading any of them:
  ctx_execute(language: "javascript", code: `
    const fs = require('fs');
    const files = fs.readdirSync('src').filter(f => f.endsWith('.ts'));
    files.forEach(f => {
      const lines = fs.readFileSync('src/'+f,'utf8').split('\\n').length;
      console.log(f + ': ' + lines + ' lines');
    });
  `)
  // 47 files analyzed, 15,314 LoC summarized — output ~3.6 KB instead of 47 Read() calls = ~700 KB.

WHEN:
  - You intend to derive an answer FROM data (filter, count, aggregate, parse, compare, transform) — do the derivation in code and print only the answer
  - Output shape or size cannot be predicted before execution (recursive finds, repo-wide greps, list endpoints, query results, log scans)
  - You would otherwise read raw output and then mentally compute — that compute belongs here, in code, where its inputs stay out of your conversation
  - You need to keep a long-running process alive (dev server, watcher, daemon) — pass `background: true` to detach on timeout instead of killing the process
  - The output may legitimately be large but you only want recall-by-topic later — pass an `intent` string; outputs over ~5KB are auto-indexed into the knowledge base and only the section titles + previews come back, retrievable via ctx_search

WHEN NOT:
  - Single observational command whose entire short output you intend to consume verbatim (whoami, pwd, git status on a clean tree) — Bash is simpler
  - File mutations (Edit/Write) or navigation (cd/ls) — Bash is the right surface
  - You already know the output is one short fixed line and you want to read it as-is

RETURNS:
  Only what your code prints. Wrap risky calls in try/catch — uncaught errors go to stderr and may leak more than intended. When `intent` is set and output exceeds the auto-index threshold, the response carries searchable section titles + previews instead of the raw stdout; use ctx_search(queries: [...]) to drill into specific sections.

EXAMPLE: ctx_execute(language: "shell", code: "npm test 2>&1 | grep -E '(FAIL|✗|×|Error:|Tests +.*(failed|passed))' | head -60")
EXAMPLE: ctx_execute(language: "javascript", code: "const out = require('child_process').execSync('gh issue list --json number,title --limit 100', {encoding:'utf8'}); const hooks = JSON.parse(out).filter(i => /hook|routing/i.test(i.title)); console.log(`${hooks.length} hook-related issues`)")

